---
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Ascii from '../components/Ascii.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="What this notebook is, and what it deliberately leaves out.">
  <div class="window window-shadow" style="max-width: 52rem; margin-inline: auto;">
    <div class="title-bar title-bar--active">
      <span class="close-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h1 class="title">About this notebook</h1>
      <span class="zoom-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="window-body">
      <div class="prose">
        <Ascii piece="typewriter" caption="everything here was typed at least twice" />

        <p>
          This is a working notebook. It collects things learned while doing the job —
          standing up servers, moving things around on disk, wiring services together,
          writing and testing web applications — with the boring parts (the ones where
          nothing goes wrong) left out.
        </p>

        <p>
          The rule for a note to exist: it has to contain at least one thing that was
          <em>not</em> obvious, and that I got wrong before getting right. Documentation
          is easy to find; the reason a command failed at 2am is not.
        </p>

        <h2>What is deliberately missing</h2>
        <p>
          Everything that identifies a person or a customer. No real names, no company or
          product names, no hostnames, no IP addresses, no keys, no paths with a username
          in them, no ticket numbers, no screenshots of real data. Where a story needs a
          subject it says “a franchise ordering portal” or “a mini-PC with four SATA
          drives” instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is enforced, not promised: <code>npm run anon</code> scans the sources and
          the built output for the shapes of private data (emails, IPs, credentials,
          personal paths) and for a local, git-ignored list of terms, and fails the build
          if any of them appear. The gate is described in full on the
          <a href={url('/colophon/')}>colophon</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>House style</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Commands are shown exactly as they were run, including the flags that mattered.</li>
          <li>Error strings are quoted verbatim — they are how you find this page later.</li>
          <li>
            Each note carries one picture — a photograph decoded to ascii, chosen to suit the
            subject — hung on the desktop behind the window rather than framed at the top of the
            post.
          </li>
          <li>
            The site looks like a machine: windows on a System 7 desktop, one-bit chrome,
            the menu bar in Chicago — but the reading is modern, body text in your own
            system's UI font, and colour kept where it carries meaning (links, the active
            window, tags) rather than sprayed over the page.
          </li>
          <li>Dates are pinned to UTC, so a page means the same thing wherever it is built.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          There is no contact form and no analytics. The site is static: a build produces
          HTML, and that is what you were served. {SITE.title} is written by
          {SITE.author}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
